# §2. Correction to AO48-DER-037 — owned

**Error #12 (methodological): power-law attribution from a single d.**
DER-037 §3 claimed the axial relative coefficient was "1/d², measured to
0.6%." At d = 0.1, 1/d² = 100 and the true law 9.93/d = 99.3 are
indistinguishable — a one-point coincidence I mistook for confirmation.
Kimi's three-point measurement exposes it; my re-run confirms it. The
corrected stratum laws: **axis c1 = −Θ(d⁷), B ≈ 9.93/d; generic
B ≈ 2.39/d; transverse c1 ≡ 0 exactly.** The transverse-1/d prediction
in WO-038 is likewise withdrawn.

**What stands:** Lemma 1 (additive uniform) untouched. Lemma 2 stands as
an upper bound (10/d ≤ C/d² throughout the range) — but it is **not
saturated on the axis**: the true first-order coefficient carries one
more factor of d than the Cauchy–Schwarz row bound allows for. Open
mechanism question, flagged for any line: why is Cov(G₁, G₁′ | pins)
= O(d³) rather than the schematic O(d²)? (Candidate: a midpoint-
reflection cancellation in the odd sector.)

**Transverse parity, upgraded to an exact lemma (their argument,
verified):** the reflection (x,y,z) → (x,−y,z) preserves the field law
and the pin set, and conjugates Cov(∇f(M+ρe₂)|pins) by diag(1,−1,1);
hence det C(ρ, e₂) is even in ρ and c1 ≡ 0 identically. The transverse
face's relative remainder is Θ((ρ/d)²) — benign on the whole collar.

